{; The retained block vocabulary belongs to in-memory render values and stable
 ; DOM ids. Namespace-page membership and ordering come from `render.walk`;
 ; nothing here declares or stores an agent-owned block set.
 :seon.render.block/name :keyword

 ; Temporary dependency of the in-flight context-contribution schema. The
 ; context lane removes both together; no render code reads this attribute.
 :seon.render.block/band [:enum :anchor :program :authored :continuity :dynamic]

 ; An in-memory named render declaration accepted by the retained direct
 ; `unit`/`surface` helpers. It is not a database entity family.
 :seon.render.block/block
 [:map
  [:seon.render.block/name :seon.render.block/name]
  [:seon.render/ai {:optional true} :seon.render/ai]
  [:seon.render/html {:optional true} :seon.render/html]]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE HTML KIND
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; One more kind on the open set. Its consumer is a surface in the
 ; browser, and its output grammar is hiccup — stated by the consumer,
 ; per the router's rule that a kind's grammar belongs to the kind's
 ; consumer and never to the router.
 :seon.render/html :seon.render/projection

 ; HICCUP IS A NAMED PREDICATE, and that is a limitation being paid for
 ; honestly rather than a preference. Hiccup is a tree, and a
 ; self-referential declaration currently overflows the stack in
 ; `seon.schema/bind-predicates` (issue
 ; a-self-referential-schema-overflows-the-stack). The supported idiom
 ; for a shape this registry cannot express is a registered core
 ; predicate with an honest generator, which is strictly better than
 ; `:any` here anyway: the grammar belongs to the serializer that
 ; enforces it, the predicate IS that grammar, and the generator's
 ; output domain is the serializer's input domain so every totality
 ; property generates against the real thing.
 :seon.render/hiccup [:fn {:gen/gen seon.render.hiccup/hiccup-generator} seon.render.hiccup/hiccup?]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE RENDERED PAGE
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; The DOM element id, derived from the name by ONE function so the hole
 ; and the patch can never drift apart. `surface-` prefixed because the
 ; id shares a document with ids nobody here owns.
 :seon.render/surface-id [:string {:min 9}]

 ; ONE RENDERED BLOCK. Success carries the output; failure carries the
 ; flat error value INSTEAD — never merged beside it, because
 ; `:seon.error/value` is a closed shape registered once and a map that
 ; is both is neither. The surface always carries its name and id, so a
 ; failed block still has a place to put its error card and its
 ; neighbours are unaffected: per-block isolation is structural here, not
 ; a try/catch somebody remembered.
 ;
 ; A UNION OF TWO CLOSED MAPS rather than one map with two optional keys,
 ; and no discriminator: closedness already makes the two disjoint, and
 ; `:seon.error/value` presence is the same "did this render?" test the
 ; router's own wrap contract established. This is not the `:close`
 ; precedent — there the FOLD's end had to be visible in the value
 ; because an absent ordinal meant two different things; here nothing is
 ; inferred from an absence, only read from a presence.
 :seon.render/surface
 [:or
  [:map {:closed true}
   [:seon.render.block/name :seon.render.block/name]
   [:seon.render/surface-id :seon.render/surface-id]
   [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
   [:seon.render/output :seon.render/output]]
  [:map {:closed true}
   [:seon.render.block/name :seon.render.block/name]
   [:seon.render/surface-id :seon.render/surface-id]
   [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
   [:seon.error/value :seon.error/value]]]

 ; Rendered values supplied to the retained bounded slot expander.
 :seon.render/surfaces [:vector :seon.render/surface]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; DISTANCE — how many hops of the neighborhood one render reaches
 ; (owner ruling, 2026-07-28 post-midnight; seal revision to the request
 ; shapes below).
 ;
 ; NO NEW NOUN AND NO NEW KIND: distance is a REQUEST PARAMETER threaded
 ; to the renderers and to the one bounded expansion, exactly as caps and
 ; the trusted inputs already are. "The neighborhood view" is therefore
 ; the same page mechanism asked a different question, not a second one.
 ;
 ; THE CALL CONVENTION, and it is the whole design: distance is an
 ; argument TO the renderer, never a property OF it. The renderer is
 ; CALLED with the distance (present on the unit; IMPLIED 1 when absent,
 ; and never required — a projection that ignores it is correct).
 ; Reaching past its own unit is the renderer'S OWN COMPOSITIONAL ACT: it
 ; includes a neighbor by DELEGATING to that neighbor's renderer —
 ; mechanically, by emitting the ref hole `seon.render.block/entity-slot`
 ; already produces — and the expansion DECREMENTS distance per hop, so
 ; the neighbor is rendered at distance-1 and distance 0 is identity
 ; only: no expansion at all.
 ;
 ; REDIRECTION AT THE SLOT: a ref hole may carry an explicit projection
 ; symbol, and the neighbor is then rendered through that projection
 ; instead of its own declaration — the "override at any point" half of
 ; the ruling, routed through the ONE router like every other
 ; declaration, and recorded where every projection choice is recorded:
 ; in the emitting renderer's own output.
 ;
 ; ACCRETION, strictly. Absent means exactly today's behaviour: the
 ; admission caps alone bound the walk. Distance never overrides those
 ; caps — the depth and node dials remain the absolute bound, and
 ; distance SELECTS WITHIN them.
 :seon.render/distance [:int {:min 0}]

 ; The retained direct unit builder's input.
 :seon.render/unit-request
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.db/db :seon.db/database-value]
  [:seon.cluster.agent/id :seon.cluster.agent/id]
  [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps]
  [:seon.render/distance {:optional true} :seon.render/distance]
  [:seon.ai/partial {:optional true} :seon.ai/partial]
  [:seon.cluster.run/live-processes {:optional true}
   :seon.cluster.run/live-processes]]

 ; What one bounded expansion needs. The database value is OPTIONAL
 ; because block-slot expansion needs none; an entity ref without one is
 ; refused in place, naming what it needed, rather than silently
 ; rendering an empty hole.
 ;
 ; DISTANCE is optional: absent means the caps alone bound the expansion;
 ; present means each ref hop spends one.
 :seon.render/expansion
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.render/surfaces :seon.render/surfaces]
  [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps]
  [:seon.render/distance {:optional true} :seon.render/distance]
  [:seon.db/db {:optional true} :seon.db/database-value]]

 ; The shell's ordered hiccup fragment.
 :seon.render/page [:vector :seon.render/hiccup]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; GENERIC DEFAULT + SPECIALIST — the reusable selection shape
 ; (owner architectural ruling, 2026-07-27 night)
 ;
 ; Every output kind has a GENERIC default renderer that can project any
 ; value of the family. A producer that knows more about a particular
 ; value points that value's key at a SPECIALIST instead — and the
 ; choice is made WHERE THE UNIT IS BUILT, computed from the value's own
 ; attributes.
 ;
 ; The two halves of the ruling are one design. Because the key on the
 ; unit is the whole answer, a CONSUMER never branches: it hands the
 ; unit to the one router and receives whatever the producer decided.
 ; That is what makes "consumers reach an error's renderings only
 ; through the router" enforceable rather than a convention — there is
 ; no second call site to reach for, because the specialist's name never
 ; leaves the producer.
 ;
 ; It also keeps hot reload: the specialist is a SYMBOL resolved late by
 ; the router on every render, so re-evaluating it changes the next
 ; render, exactly as the generic default does.
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; ONE RULE: a pure predicate over the value's own attributes, and the
 ; projection to use when it accepts. Both are qualified symbols
 ; resolved late — a rule is as hot-reloadable as the renderer it picks.
 :seon.render/specialist [:tuple :qualified-symbol :seon.render/projection]

 ; What a producer asks for. `specialists` is ORDERED and first match
 ; wins, because "more specific" is a judgement its owner makes and not
 ; something a scoring function should guess. An empty vector is the
 ; ordinary case and yields the default, so a family with no specialists
 ; writes no special code.
 ;
 ; This is NOT a registry: the vector belongs to the ONE producer that
 ; owns the family, is built from that family's own attributes, and is
 ; never consulted by anybody rendering. A second family's rules are
 ; invisible here, which is the difference between a producer's rules
 ; and a dispatch table.
 :seon.render/selection
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
  [:seon.render/default :seon.render/projection]
  [:seon.render/specialists [:vector :seon.render/specialist]]]}
